The features of the model:
  The features of my model are the costumers age, annual income, household size, when the costumer started shopping with the grocery
  store, how recently they purchased from the store and how much they have spent on wine products in the past.

The label of the model:
  The label of my model is the characteristics of a costumer who is most likely to be persuaded by a marketing campaign for a new wine 
  section. 

The training data:
  The training data would contain 75% of the data from the data set so the rest of the data could be used to test the model. 

The testing data:
  The testing data needs to be kept seperate because if you try to test the model with the data you have already given it it will
  automatically know if it is correct or not since we have already given it the answer.

False positives:
  A false positive would be identifying someone as likely to purchase the wine when they are not actually very likely to.

False negative:
  A false negative would be identifying someone as not likely to buy wine when they would be likely to buy from the new wine section.

Percision vs. Recall:
  Recall would be more important compared to precision since this is not something that would harm the company too much if they
  advertise to more people than will actually be interested. This is because even if someone is not interested in the wine they may be
  interested in the rest of the store and the advertising may still work.

Poor quality data:
  If people have lied about their age or if the data is wrong in other aspects it will cause the model to believe certain people are
  going to be identified as likely to buy wine even if they are not. This will cause the grocery store to advertise to people who will
  not or can not buy from the new wine section.

Overfitting:
  If the model recognizes patterns that do not actually affect how likely someone is to buy wine it may use this to determine how 
  likely someone is to purchase alcohol even though it is not a real factor.

Concerns:
  If the model started to give predictions of costumers who I know would not buy the wine. This could include people under the age of
  twenty-one, someone who has never spent money on wine before, or someone who is way different from the other predictions made.
